B.NR=new A.dV("Loose leash walking \u2014 generalization check","gen-loose-leash","Your dog should have learned that a loose leash produces forward motion, a tight leash stops forward motion, and that treats are available at your side. Have you accomplished this:",B.VC,null)
B.Yq=s([B.NW,B.O4,B.NQ,B.NT,B.O2,B.O6,B.NY,B.NR],t.e7)
B.Pw=new A.pf("Week 6",6,'In our time together, you have been working on the following behaviors with your dog:\n\n- Name recognition / attention\n- Hand-targeting\n- Leave it\n- Go to your mat\n- Down\n- Sit\n- Loose-leash walking\n- Recall\n- Stay\n\nYou have used food as a reinforcer. You may have also used toys and your voice ("good dog!"). You have (hopefully) practiced these behaviors in a few different locations. Your dog\'s continued success depends on a process called **"generalization"** \u2014 using the behaviors in *new* environments.\n\nThis week is a self-check: tick off each scenario your dog can do.\n',B.Yq,'## Fading the clicker and "thinning" the schedule of reinforcement\n\nThe clicker is great for teaching new behaviors, but we do not have to rely on it to tell our dogs they performed correctly. Pair a word, such as **"Yes!"** or **"Good!"** with a treat, and practice using your word in the same way you used the clicker.\n\nYou should also gradually decrease how often you reinforce a behavior. Become *less predictable*. Instead of reinforcing with the click or marker/food or praise/feed every time, use this schedule:\n\n> Every 2nd or 3rd time, e.g., dog takes two or three steps on a loose leash, reinforce after the second or third step.\n\nWhen this produces reliable results, become even less predictable, using a schedule like this:\n\n> Every 2, 1, 3, 4, 2, 6, 3, 2, 5, 2, 1, 6, 5, 3, 3, 1, 5, 5, 2, 1, 6\n\nThis will cause your dog to work harder than ever. Use this type of schedule for loose-leash walking and duration of sits and downs.\n\nAlso, begin to use praise *without* giving a treat sometimes. Over time, praise will become what your dog works for. Do not rush this process, or your dog\'s behavior may break down. If, at any time, your dog seems confused or does not do what you are training, go back to the last correct performance and work there for a bit. People will announce *"My dog KNOWS this!"* when their dog does not do what they ask. The truth is \u2014 if you have taught a behavior and practiced it in lots of places, you will see consistent results. **If your dog does not do what you asked, you are not done teaching it.**\n\nYou should also use things in the environment as rewards. Reward your dog\'s sit with unclipping the leash in the (fenced-in) park, or by throwing the ball, or a stick. A great reward for sitting at the door is\u2026 *the door opens!*\n\nChanging behavior is a lifelong process, and it is *not* a linear one. You will have good days, and not-so-good days. Continue to teach in short sessions, and in small increments, while taking every opportunity all day long to *catch* your dog being "good" and letting them know you noticed, via a treat, a toy, petting, or praise.\n\nReview your data sheets, and keep up the good work in the weeks, months, and years to come \u2014 and don\'t forget about *"grad" school* for your dog; many dogs love to keep learning!\n')
